The '''Badweyn Islands''' were a Burawi colonial territory in the [[Capuyaquiran Sea]] that administered a group of islands now divided among the [[United Capuyaquiran Republic]], [[Narubi]], and the [[Bolodun and Kuvari Islands]]. The territory was named after the Burawi explorer [[Guled Badweyn]], who passed through the archipelago during his 1520 expedition and recorded the islands for Burawi authorities. The name was initially geographic and administrative rather than political: the islands continued to be governed from the [[Lacashe]] province of [[New Burawa]] after their discovery.

The islands became a formal territorial possession after the [[First Capuyaquiran War]] (1540–1543), when Burawa and its Guajicutean allies defeated several former tributaries of [[Guajicutea]]. The new territory remained under the government of New Burawa in Lacashe for most of its early history. Following the [[Third Capuyaquiran War]] (1578–1581), the Burawi government reorganised the archipelago and appointed a governor of the Badweyn Islands, giving the territory a separate colonial administration. The governor's seat was later established on [[Bolodun]] in 1695.

The territory was dissolved during the political changes that followed the [[Burawi Imperial Split]] and the formation of the [[Capuyaquiran League]] in 1755. Its islands subsequently followed different political paths, including incorporation into the United Capuyaquiran Republic, the independence of Narubi, and the later state formation of the Bolodun and Kuvari Islands.

== History ==
=== Discovery and early administration ===

Guled Badweyn encountered the archipelago during his 1520 voyage from the Burawi colony of Lacashe. His expedition passed west through the islands before reaching Guajicutea later that year. Burawi records used ''Badweyn Islands'' as a convenient geographic designation, referring to the islands as a group associated with the explorer's route. The designation did not initially imply a separate colony or governorate.

For the first decades after discovery, the islands were administered through Lacashe. Burawi officials in New Burawa treated the archipelago as part of the wider Capuyaquiran sphere, relying on local rulers, trade networks, and naval expeditions rather than maintaining a unified island government. The arrangement allowed Burawa to supervise trade and missionary activity while limiting the cost of direct administration.

=== Formal territorial organisation ===

The [[First Capuyaquiran War]] brought the islands into a more direct colonial framework. Between 1540 and 1543, Murani, Dukuri, and Barani polities that had previously been tributaries of Guajicutea attempted to recover their independence. Burawi forces, supported by Guajicutean soldiers and sailors, defeated the revolt and incorporated the participating islands as overseas territories under Burawi authority. The conquered islands were collectively organised as the Badweyn Islands, although their local governments and customary institutions continued to operate under colonial supervision.

The territory remained formally subordinate to the government in New Burawa at Lacashe. The provincial administration appointed officials, supervised customs and military affairs, and coordinated missionary activity. Local rulers retained authority over village administration and landholding where they accepted Burawi sovereignty. The territory's administrative centre was associated with the Lacashe system rather than with a permanent island capital.

=== Reorganisation after the Third Capuyaquiran War ===

Burawa's victory in the [[Third Capuyaquiran War]] (1578–1581) expanded its control over the remaining islands of Greater Capuyaquiran. The enlarged colonial sphere made administration through Lacashe increasingly impractical. Burawi authorities therefore created a separate governorship for the Badweyn Islands after the war, while retaining the territory's name and its connection to the earlier colonial system.

The governor exercised authority over defence, customs, inter-island shipping, and relations with local rulers. The office also coordinated the movement of enslaved people, plantation goods, soldiers, and missionaries through the archipelago. The territory's strategic position made it a staging area for Burawi naval operations and a link between the Lacashe colony, Guajicutea, and the eastern Capuyaquiran islands.

In 1695, the colonial government established the governor's seat on Bolodun. The decision reflected the island's agricultural and naval importance and reduced the administration's dependence on Lacashe. Bolodun developed as the principal administrative and plantation centre, while other islands retained distinct local economies and political arrangements.

=== Dissolution ===

The Badweyn Islands governorship weakened during the eighteenth century as the territory's isolation from the Kaftian peninsula increased and island communities developed stronger local institutions. The [[Burawi Imperial Split]] of 1709 divided the former empire among the Kingdom of Burawa, the Kingdom of Galwa, and autonomous Lacashe. The Badweyn Islands were retained by the [[Kingdom of Burawa]], the eastern successor state, which inherited the imperial capital and the greater part of the naval establishment. The islands remained a Burawi colonial possession for the following decades, though the metropolitan government's capacity to administer them declined steadily.

The [[Capuyaquiran League]] established in 1755 ended the remaining colonial framework in the archipelago. The former Badweyn Islands were not transferred as a single successor state. Instead, individual islands and island groups entered the League, retained separate governments, or later formed independent states. This division accounts for the territory's modern distribution among the United Capuyaquiran Republic, Narubi, and the Bolodun and Kuvari Islands.

== Government ==

During its early period, the Badweyn Islands were administered as an overseas dependency of the Lacashe province of New Burawa. The provincial government appointed officials and handled military, commercial, and diplomatic matters. Local authorities continued to manage customary law, agricultural production, and village affairs subject to Burawi oversight.

After the creation of the governorship, the governor became the highest colonial authority in the territory. The administration maintained garrisons and ports, collected customs duties, supervised plantation districts, and regulated inter-island shipping. The governor's office did not eliminate local rulers, but placed them within a hierarchy of colonial officials and military commanders.

== Legacy ==

The name Badweyn Islands remains in historical and geographic usage for the former Burawi island territory. It is also used to describe the shared colonial history of islands that now belong to several states. Modern political boundaries do not correspond exactly to the former governorship, whose extent changed as Burawi authority expanded and contracted during the sixteenth and eighteenth centuries.

